WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.200
-Our next guest is
a Tony Award-winning actress

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:04.567
you know from her work
on Broadway

00:00:04.567 --> 00:00:06.000
and "Sunday in the Park
with George"

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.334
and "You Can't Take It
with You,"

00:00:07.334 --> 00:00:09.267
and shows such as "Master
of Sex" and "B Positive."

00:00:09.267 --> 00:00:12.801
She stars in the highly
anticipated revival of

00:00:12.801 --> 00:00:15.100
"Sweeney Todd: The Demon Barber
of Fleet Street,"

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.601
which is now in previews
at the Lunt-Fontanne Theatre.

00:00:17.601 --> 00:00:18.801
Please welcome back to the show

00:00:18.801 --> 00:00:20.968
our friend Annaleigh Ashford,
everybody.

00:00:20.968 --> 00:00:22.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.901 --> 00:00:29.400
♪♪

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:35.901
♪♪

00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:37.901
Hello.
-Hello.

00:00:37.901 --> 00:00:41.467
-I'm so happy to have you back.
-I'm so happy to be back.

00:00:41.467 --> 00:00:42.734
-We've talked in the past.

00:00:42.734 --> 00:00:43.834
You were here talking about
"Sunday in the Park

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:45.767
with George,"
which is also Sondheim.

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.901
And now you're doing Sondheim
again,

00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:51.567
but very sadly,
we lost Stephen in 2021.

00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:53.868
And I'm wondering,
how often do you think

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.200
about him in the process

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.200
of doing rehearsals
and previews for the show?

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:59.567
-I mean, every moment.

00:00:59.567 --> 00:01:01.267
Every time I get to a new song,

00:01:01.267 --> 00:01:03.634
I sort of think of it
as its own little puzzle,

00:01:03.634 --> 00:01:07.033
which is very apropos
because he loved puzzles.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:10.200
And so I kind of just
go to a little spot up in heaven

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:13.067
and say, "Hey. Let's go do a
puzzle together. Here we go."

00:01:13.067 --> 00:01:16.567
You know. Kind of helps me
get through the show, too.

00:01:16.567 --> 00:01:19.334
-Now, for those who don't know
about "Sweeney Todd,"

00:01:19.334 --> 00:01:23.167
this is about a -- sort of
a revenge-seeking story.

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.701
Will you try your best

00:01:24.701 --> 00:01:26.934
to explain it
to those who are uninformed?

00:01:26.934 --> 00:01:28.667
-Yeah, so,
it's about this guy who --

00:01:28.667 --> 00:01:30.434
[Valley Girl accent]
He's from London.

00:01:30.434 --> 00:01:31.767
It's about this guy.

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:34.334
[ Laughter ]

00:01:34.334 --> 00:01:37.400
He's from Londontown,
and, um, he, like --

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.234
He, like, goes back to London
to, like, seek revenge,

00:01:40.234 --> 00:01:43.033
and he kills lots of people
and puts them into pies!

00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.033
-He puts them into pies.
-He puts them into pies.

00:01:45.033 --> 00:01:48.234
-It's a very bloody show.
-[Normal voice] Yeah.

00:01:48.234 --> 00:01:51.701
-It's sort of like --
It's both a renowned musical,

00:01:51.701 --> 00:01:54.133
and it's a horror movie,
as well.

00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:56.167
-Yes.
I love that Stephen Sondheim

00:01:56.167 --> 00:01:58.100
used to say,
"Well, it's a horror musical.

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.601
It's the only one of its kind."

00:01:59.601 --> 00:02:01.300
There's, like,
no other horror musical.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.734
-There's no other
horror musical.

00:02:02.734 --> 00:02:05.000
And does this bring
a different sort of audience?

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:06.868
Have you found the reaction
to it is different

00:02:06.868 --> 00:02:08.501
than, say,
when you were doing "Sunday"?

00:02:08.501 --> 00:02:11.367
-You know, there's a --
[ Laughs ]

00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:13.267
There's a magic right
at the top of the show

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:16.100
that is this, like,
undeniable scream,

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:18.334
right when you hear the organ,
and it feels like,

00:02:18.334 --> 00:02:19.834
"Are we, like --

00:02:19.834 --> 00:02:22.300
Are we at, like, one of those
scary movies on opening night?

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.400
Like, where we're gonna
grab each other?"

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.868
It's kind of thrilling.
It's wild.

00:02:26.868 --> 00:02:29.968
I'm like, "Do you people know
you're at a musical?

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:32.667
Get ready. here we go."

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:35.334
-It's very physical, as well.
It's very funny, very physical.

00:02:35.334 --> 00:02:37.934
And the physical-comedy element,

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:40.133
I'm wondering, when you're
rehearsing things like that,

00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:41.801
you do have to be
fairly precise with it.

00:02:41.801 --> 00:02:43.601
Has anything gone wrong
in the previews?

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:45.167
-Things go wrong
all of the time.

00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:46.400
-Okay.
You know.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.701
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-You know comedy.

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:49.901
You're like, "I hope it goes
well, but it may not.

00:02:49.901 --> 00:02:51.267
So let's just see what happens."

00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:52.968
Yeah, the other night,
we actually --

00:02:52.968 --> 00:02:55.567
I jump on this little counter
in the pie shop,

00:02:55.567 --> 00:02:57.901
and I jumped,
and I didn't make it.

00:02:57.901 --> 00:03:00.100
-You didn't make it up
to the top?

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:01.567
-No. And then I --

00:03:01.567 --> 00:03:04.334
-Wait. What happens when
you jump on a counter and miss?

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:06.534
-You just fall. You just fall.
-Yeah.

00:03:06.534 --> 00:03:09.100
Would you fall in a way
that the audience thinks

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.133
it's planned
or is it very clear?

00:03:11.133 --> 00:03:13.133
-The audience went --
[ Gasps ]

00:03:13.133 --> 00:03:14.834
[ Laughter ]

00:03:14.834 --> 00:03:17.267
I mean, that's happened
to me on stage before.

00:03:17.267 --> 00:03:18.701
Has it happened to you?

00:03:18.701 --> 00:03:20.567
-I don't know
if I've taken a fall.

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:22.934
I don't -- I mean,
I think early in my career,

00:03:22.934 --> 00:03:24.934
no director
would let me do any jumping.

00:03:24.934 --> 00:03:27.400
I think the very fact
that they trust you with that

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.133
speaks more highly
of your stage craft.

00:03:29.133 --> 00:03:30.501
-I mean, what I would say is,

00:03:30.501 --> 00:03:32.734
"If you fall, just tell
the audience you're okay."

00:03:32.734 --> 00:03:35.634
'Cause I feel like they spent
the next 30 seconds

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:37.300
being like, "Is she okay?"

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.267
So I ran off stage.

00:03:39.267 --> 00:03:42.501
I did a lot of, you know,
jumping and dancing.

00:03:42.501 --> 00:03:44.834
I did a backwards somersault.
It was very uncomfortable.

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:47.234
For everybody. Including myself.

00:03:47.234 --> 00:03:48.801
I'm, fine though. I'm fine.

00:03:48.801 --> 00:03:51.000
-Your son is six and a half.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.400
-Yeah.

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.000
-Now, he saw his first Sondheim
musical. Is that correct?

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.300
-He did. We got to New York.

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.434
We had been in L.A.
for the last couple years,

00:03:58.434 --> 00:04:00.400
and we got to new York,
and I was like,

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:01.868
"He needs to see
a Broadway show."

00:04:01.868 --> 00:04:03.033
He's old enough now."

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:05.934
And he's not gonna see mine.

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:07.434
Because it's a horror musical.

00:04:07.434 --> 00:04:09.067
-Right.
-So it's not because of you.

00:04:09.067 --> 00:04:10.801
He would be fine
seeing you on stage.

00:04:10.801 --> 00:04:12.400
You're just in one
that's inappropriate.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:14.701
-I hope so. I hope he would be
okay seeing me on stage.

00:04:14.701 --> 00:04:16.534
You never know. You never know.

00:04:16.534 --> 00:04:19.167
Yeah, so, we took him
to "Into the Woods,"

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:20.567
which I thought was perfect.

00:04:20.567 --> 00:04:23.133
'Cause it's Sondheim, and I just
wanted that to be his first one.

00:04:23.133 --> 00:04:25.300
And he loved it.
-That's so exciting.

00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:27.334
-Yeah, he knew
what was happening.

00:04:27.334 --> 00:04:29.434
-Were you checking in with him
constantly?

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:31.300
-Yes, to the point
where he was like,

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.300
"I know what's happening, Mom.
Back off." You know?

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:36.701
-That's really adorable.

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:39.000
Because, as I was telling you
backstage, my son,

00:04:39.000 --> 00:04:41.133
who's about the same age,
went with my wife,

00:04:41.133 --> 00:04:43.400
and I think he was totally lost,
so...

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.067
Again, it's not a contest.

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:49.000
People, you know, develop
at different speeds.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:50.868
-Did he like the giant?
-He liked the giant.

00:04:50.868 --> 00:04:53.334
-They all love the giant.
-Yeah, they all love the giant.

00:04:53.334 --> 00:04:55.067
-Yeah.
-That's what he came home --

00:04:55.067 --> 00:04:58.133
He failed to answer any of
my follow-ups about the giant.

00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:00.100
He's like, "There was a giant."
I'm like, "Tell me more."

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.767
He's like, "Dad, go see
your own Broadway shows."

00:05:02.767 --> 00:05:04.834
[ Laughter ]

00:05:04.834 --> 00:05:06.934
Again, bloody, gory.

00:05:06.934 --> 00:05:10.267
How -- I would imagine
there's so many logistics

00:05:10.267 --> 00:05:12.501
to a show like this with
the sort of special effects

00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:14.267
that you're not used to
in a Broadway show.

00:05:14.267 --> 00:05:17.200
-There's so much blood.
That's one kind of fun thing.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:19.267
You know when there's, like,

00:05:19.267 --> 00:05:22.367
glitter in your
child's class project

00:05:22.367 --> 00:05:24.667
or an outfit you're wearing --
I don't know.

00:05:24.667 --> 00:05:27.367
Whatever you're using glitter
for. And you find it forever?

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:28.834
-Yeah.
-You know, they would say like,

00:05:28.834 --> 00:05:30.434
"Glitter is like herpes."

00:05:30.434 --> 00:05:32.400
Well -- Isn't that a thing?

00:05:32.400 --> 00:05:33.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.934 --> 00:05:36.367
-The glitter stores
don't say that.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:38.033
[ Laughter ]

00:05:38.033 --> 00:05:40.000
Yeah.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.767
Like, no one at the ad agency
was like, "We got it!"

00:05:41.767 --> 00:05:43.667
-No.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:43.667 --> 00:05:44.834
Do you know what else somebody
--

00:05:44.834 --> 00:05:46.467
Our choreographer
said the other day,

00:05:46.467 --> 00:05:49.300
"You know, glitter is --
Glitter are like pubes.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:05:50.734
You find them everywhere."

00:05:50.734 --> 00:05:54.167
You were like, "Do you?"

00:05:54.167 --> 00:05:56.934
-Nothing's worse than when
you make a big bold statement

00:05:56.934 --> 00:05:59.200
like that and immediately
realize -- You're like,

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.534
"I might have a different
experience with pubes."

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:04.133
[ Laughter ]
-I mean --

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:06.734
And also when you find glitter,
you're like, "I found glitter!"

00:06:06.734 --> 00:06:09.033
When you find a pube, you're not
like, "I found a pube!"

00:06:09.033 --> 00:06:11.567
-Yeah.

00:06:11.567 --> 00:06:13.400
-Also, with glitter,
you know where it came from.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:14.534
I mean -- You're like -- Yeah.

00:06:14.534 --> 00:06:17.901
"Is this one mine?
I mean, is it...?"

00:06:17.901 --> 00:06:22.934
-"It's green. It's not silver.
Where'd it come from? Aah!"

00:06:22.934 --> 00:06:25.067
But, yeah, what I was
leading to is, you know,

00:06:25.067 --> 00:06:28.334
I find a lot of blood in places
that I would least expect it.

00:06:28.334 --> 00:06:29.934
And 'cause, you know,
stage blood

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:31.133
is like glitter and pubes.

00:06:31.133 --> 00:06:32.667
You find it everywhere.

00:06:32.667 --> 00:06:34.467
-You -- That old Broadway adage.

00:06:34.467 --> 00:06:36.133
-Aww.

00:06:36.133 --> 00:06:37.534
[ Laughter ]

00:06:37.534 --> 00:06:39.667
-This is -- First of all,
I mostly just want

00:06:39.667 --> 00:06:42.467
to thank you for being here,
because I did not realize
you're in previews,

00:06:42.467 --> 00:06:44.267
so you're doing basically --
Again --

00:06:44.267 --> 00:06:46.067
It's just a Broadway show.
It's just before it opens.

00:06:46.067 --> 00:06:47.367
You're doing the whole thing.
-Yeah.

00:06:47.367 --> 00:06:48.667
-But, also,
when you're in previews,

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:49.934
you're rehearsing all day,
as well.

00:06:49.934 --> 00:06:51.968
-I was literally
just at rehearsal

00:06:51.968 --> 00:06:54.400
in a weird sweatsuit outfit
that I should have --

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:58.300
[Dramatically] And then look.
I'm going to the Met Gala now.

00:06:58.300 --> 00:07:00.200
Gonna lay down on this couch.

00:07:00.200 --> 00:07:02.300
[Normal voice] But, yeah, I'm
about to go put on my sweatsuit

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.234
and then go back to the theater,
we'll run some stuff.

00:07:04.234 --> 00:07:06.300
Like, the orchestra
comes at 6:30 tonight

00:07:06.300 --> 00:07:07.501
to work on some things.

00:07:07.501 --> 00:07:09.100
But it's crazy.
It's a crazy thing.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.701
-So then -- I think we all --

00:07:11.701 --> 00:07:13.267
For the laymen like myself,

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:15.000
we think of,
"Oh, my God. Opening.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:16.267
It must get so stressful then."

00:07:16.267 --> 00:07:18.133
But it must be such a relief
when you open.

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:19.834
-Oh! I remember
another show that I --

00:07:19.834 --> 00:07:21.400
I did a show called
"Kinky Boots."

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:23.734
And I remember the night
after opening being like,

00:07:23.734 --> 00:07:26.000
"This is the best show
of my life!"

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:27.667
And everybody's like,
"What? What's going on?"

00:07:27.667 --> 00:07:29.767
I was like, "We opened!
We did it. We already did it.

00:07:29.767 --> 00:07:31.167
And now we get to do the show."

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:32.734
There's kind of this
magical release.

00:07:32.734 --> 00:07:34.767
-Yeah, it must be just a really
nice day when all you have to do

00:07:34.767 --> 00:07:36.167
is focus on doing a show

00:07:36.167 --> 00:07:37.534
for an audience
that's excited to see it.

00:07:37.534 --> 00:07:38.634
-That's it.
-Yeah.

00:07:38.634 --> 00:07:39.934
-That's all
you have to worry about.

00:07:39.934 --> 00:07:41.334
-Well, I'm very excited

00:07:41.334 --> 00:07:43.367
that you're on the cusp of that
very soon.

00:07:43.367 --> 00:07:45.200
-Thank you.
-And I can't wait for it.

00:07:45.200 --> 00:07:47.200
And it's just so lovely
to have you back.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:48.968
Thank you so much.
I really appreciate it.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.734
-Thank you.
-Annaleigh Ashford, everybody.

00:07:50.734 --> 00:07:53.501
"Sweeney Todd" is in previews
at Broadway's

00:07:53.501 --> 00:07:56.267
Lunt-Fontanne Theatre and
officially opens on March 26th.

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:58.467
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

